        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-15T23:10:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What is the ownership of the returned data? Also does the storage server have to allocate a buffer to store the data for the entire read request? This will not work for some of the files we read with the current api which are much larger than the heap size in the storage server.",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a62eb1f4_a718cfb9",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/IFile.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962190
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T15:50:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In C++/Rust, a successful ReadResult will contain a std::vector/Vec that will own the data. If the storage server can\u0027t allocate all the requested data at once, the client would have to call multiple times, which IIUC is what storage server is currently doing, correct?\n\nThe other way to define `ReadFile` that could make sense is:\n\n```\nparcelable ReadResult {\n    Error error \u003d Error.GENERIC;\n    long bytes_read;\n}\n\ninterface IFile {\n    ReadResult Read(long offset, out byte[] buffer);\n}\n```\n\nbut I think the way this gets translated by the AIDL compiler is by the server allocating a buffer as big as the one the client passed as an argument, even if that\u0027s bigger than `STORAGE_MAX_BUFFER_SIZE`. If the server is creating a Vec to return instead, we get to control how big of a buffer we use instead of the aidl lib and client dictating it.",
      "parentUuid": "603e76c0_bc677177",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f05eb23_2aadcc85",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/ReadIntegrity.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 34,
      "author": {
        "id": 1809421
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T17:12:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We shouldn\u0027t have both a \"read option\" and a \"file option\" that ignore rollback. This only needs to be in one of those places. If we do have both, then we need to clarify in the doc comments what the difference is and what happens if they conflict.",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2c02f451_56e3fb66",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/ReadIntegrity.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 34,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962190
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T22:23:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The difference is that the file option is a property of the file (i.e. for `TAMPER_DETECT_IGNORE_RESET`, the filesystem must be able to notice general tampering and rollbacks, but doesn\u0027t need to be able to catch resets) and might determine where the file is located. The read option allows the client to determine what types of tampering they consider an error.\n\nSo if an app needs to know when their file has been rolled back in order to recover, but don\u0027t need to know about resets, they could make the file `TAMPER_DETECT` or `TAMPER_DETECT_IGNORE_RESET`, then open the file with the `IGNORE_RESET` read option. If they get an error value with `Tamper.ROLLBACK`, they\u0027ll know a rollback happened and can try opening again with the `IGNORE_ROLLBACK` read option.",
      "parentUuid": "3f05eb23_2aadcc85",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a6b794bf_c3ee85d5",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/ReadProperties.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 26,
      "author": {
        "id": 1809421
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T17:12:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What if `integrity \u003d\u003d ReadIntegrity.NO_TAMPER` but `report_recovery \u003d\u003d false`? Does it make sense to have separate fields?",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cc8383ee_270a96b5",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/ReadProperties.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 26,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962190
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T22:23:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree; this one doesn\u0027t make sense. Removed.",
      "parentUuid": "a6b794bf_c3ee85d5",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a673bbef_45408900",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/Tamper.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 1809421
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T17:12:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "File or file-system?",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b8214ac8_2e0e5b7c",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/Tamper.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962190
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T22:23:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could be either, but since we\u0027re returning this from `Open*` methods, I think it should be the file. REE might have reset the whole filesystem, but the client asked about a specific file/dir.\n\nDo you see a better way to phrase this?",
      "parentUuid": "a673bbef_45408900",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3c452f23_ad8f0648",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/Tamper.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T23:23:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The file-system might not be able to answer a question about a specific file. If the file-system was reset, all files are removed and any information about which files existed before is gone. This means that a request to open any file will fail after the first factory reset of any non-persistent file system. This is not a very useful feature. What would potentially be useful is to specify a version-number/reset-counter, although that could be exposed in many different ways, some without needing a specific error state.",
      "parentUuid": "b8214ac8_2e0e5b7c",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d5453e54_868d34f1",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/Tamper.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962190
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-17T20:35:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So if we\u0027re returning `RESET`/`ROLLBACK` here, it\u0027s specifically this file that\u0027s been reset/rolled back. If the whole filesytem\u0027s been tampered with, we might be returning \"File not found\" instead?",
      "parentUuid": "3c452f23_ad8f0648",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "92910e58_98983df4",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/Tamper.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-17T23:13:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No. We need to return that the file-system was reset or rollbacked if a client expects rollback protection for any file that does not exist. file-not-found, means the file never existed. The reverse is not true though, if we return that the file-system was reset or rollbacked, it does not mean the file existed before, just that it could have.",
      "parentUuid": "d5453e54_868d34f1",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d2a974b_8211dccd",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/result/Error.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 18,
      "author": {
        "id": 1809421
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T17:12:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AIDL has service-specific error code support, should we consider using that to simplify error handling? I\u0027m not sure what the idiomatic approach with AIDL is.",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "92146e54_56c7f52b",
        "filename": "security/see/storage/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/storage/result/Error.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 18,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962190
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T22:23:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looks like you just define a bunch of int constants on the interface like this\nhttp://cs/android-internal/frameworks/av/camera/aidl/android/hardware/ICameraService.aidl?l\u003d46\nand attach them when you construct an error status\nhttp://cs/android-internal/frameworks/native/libs/binder/include/binder/Status.h?rcl\u003deeae447b7baf32568765801cb304d72dadfcd739\u0026l\u003d98\n\nThat might be The Right Thing to do, but I kind of like the exhaustiveness we get with the enums and result unions more. I\u0027m going to see what errors we\u0027re currently returning from each method and see how everything looks.",
      "parentUuid": "5d2a974b_8211dccd",
      "revId": "4cf89cea4cd22a460d9d08cc21ed434c3092b63d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
